artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a two-dimensional van der Waals magnetic heterostructure consisting of a layered ferromagnet (such as Fe3GeTe2) interfaced with a nonmagnetic, antiferromagnetic, or topological partner layer. Suppose the ferromagnetic layer can undergo surface oxidation, producing an antiferromagnetic oxide shell that gives rise to an exchange-bias field H_EB and an associated exchange magnetic anisotropy K_EB, and suppose the heterostructure also supports magnetic proximity coupling at its interface producing an additional anisotropy contribution K_MP. Let the total interfacial exchange anisotropy be K_ex = K_EB + K_MP.

Determine, in a quantitative model of such a heterostructure, the dependence on K_ex (and on the underlying H_EB and K_MP) of each of the following device-level response functions:

(a) the longitudinal and transverse magnetoresistance MR(H, K_ex);
(b) the critical current density J_c(K_ex) for current-induced spin-orbit-torque magnetization switching, including its dependence on the angle between the bias field and the spin-orbit torque;
(c) the spin Seebeck coefficient S_SSE(K_ex) and its decoupling from the anomalous Nernst coefficient S_ANE(K_ex) in the presence of a temperature gradient;
(d) the efficiency of optical (helicity-dependent) control of the magnetization and of the SOT switching threshold as a function of K_ex.

Specifically, derive (or determine experimentally) how K_EB arising from surface oxidization and K_MP arising from magnetic proximity modify each of MR, J_c, S_SSE, S_ANE, and the optical switching threshold, and identify the regimes in which these exchange-anisotropy effects dominate over bulk magnetocrystalline anisotropy in van der Waals magnetic heterostructures.

